CAT = "ea3Exam"; SEG = "EXA"
# Representing a taxpayer in examinations and before Appeals.
Q = [
("The IRS sets the time and place of an examination, but under section 7605 they must be:",
 "Reasonable under the circumstances", ["Chosen by the taxpayer", "At the IRS office nearest the client", "Within 30 days of the notice"],
 "Section 7605(a) lets the IRS fix the time and place of an examination, provided they are reasonable under the circumstances."),
("Unless the taxpayer asks or the IRS gives written notice, how many inspections of a taxpayer's books may be made for one tax year?",
 "One", ["Two", "Three", "No set limit"],
 "Section 7605(b) allows only one inspection of a taxpayer's books of account for each taxable year unless the taxpayer requests otherwise or the IRS notifies the taxpayer in writing that another is needed."),
("The section 7525 practitioner privilege does NOT protect:",
 "Advice on a criminal matter", ["Advice on a civil audit issue", "Advice in a Tax Court case", "Advice on a refund claim"],
 "Section 7525 extends attorney-client confidentiality to federally authorized tax practitioners only for noncriminal tax matters before the IRS or noncriminal tax proceedings in federal court."),
("The section 7525 privilege does not cover written communications with a client about:",
 "Promoting a tax shelter", ["A pending appeal", "An installment agreement", "Filing status choices"],
 "Section 7525(b) excludes written communications in connection with promoting a person's participation in a tax shelter, meaning a plan with a significant purpose of avoiding or evading income tax."),
("In court, the burden of proof on a factual issue shifts to the IRS when the taxpayer:",
 "Offers credible evidence and cooperated", ["Files a timely Tax Court petition", "Is represented by an enrolled agent", "Disputed the issue first in Appeals"],
 "Section 7491(a) shifts the burden if the taxpayer introduces credible evidence and has met substantiation and record requirements and cooperated with reasonable IRS requests."),
("A client wants to audio record her in-person examination interview. She must:",
 "Request it 10 days ahead", ["Get the examiner's written consent", "Pay the IRS for a transcript", "Request it at the interview itself"],
 "Section 7521(a) and Publication 556 let the taxpayer record the interview on advance request, made 10 days ahead in writing, with her own equipment."),
("During an interview, a client asks to stop and consult her EA. The examiner must:",
 "Suspend the interview", ["Finish the current topic", "Proceed unless it is criminal", "Issue a summons first"],
 "Section 7521(b)(2) requires the IRS to suspend a noncriminal interview when the taxpayer clearly asks to consult a representative, even after some questions were answered."),
("An examiner believes a client's representative is causing unreasonable delay. With supervisor consent the examiner may:",
 "Contact the client directly", ["Disbar the representative at once", "Close the case unchanged", "Require Form 8821 instead"],
 "Section 7521(c) lets the employee, with the immediate supervisor's consent, notify the taxpayer directly that the representative is responsible for unreasonable delay or hindrance."),
("A client's return is being audited on the same items already examined in both prior years, with no change. She should:",
 "Ask if the exam should stop", ["Ask to close it with no change", "Ask to delay it for a year", "Ask to move it to another area"],
 "Publication 556 asks taxpayers examined on the same items in either of the 2 previous years, with no change proposed, to contact the IRS to see if the examination should be discontinued."),
("A client does not respond to a 30-day letter. The IRS will next issue:",
 "A notice of deficiency", ["A final notice of intent to levy", "A Notice of Federal Tax Lien", "A collection summons"],
 "Publication 556 says that if the taxpayer does not respond to the 30-day letter, or does not settle in Appeals, the IRS sends a 90-day letter, the notice of deficiency."),
("A notice of deficiency is addressed to a client living outside the United States. She may petition the Tax Court within:",
 "150 days", ["90 days", "120 days", "180 days"],
 "Section 6213(a) allows 150 days, rather than 90, when the notice is addressed to a person outside the United States."),
("A client's proposed change is $18,000 in tax and penalties for each of two years. To reach Appeals she may file:",
 "A small case request", ["A formal written protest", "Form 12153", "A Tax Court petition"],
 "Publication 5 allows a small case request when the total tax and penalties for each period is $25,000 or less. A formal protest is needed if any period exceeds that."),
("A formal written protest is required to reach Appeals, whatever the amount, for which case?",
 "An S corporation examination", ["An individual's $9,000 change", "An installment agreement rejection", "A levy on wages"],
 "Publication 5 requires a formal protest for partnership, S corporation, employee plan and exempt organization cases regardless of the dollar amount. Collection appeals have their own procedures."),
("Before an examination case goes to Appeals, IRS policy may require at least how much time left on the assessment statute?",
 "365 days", ["90 days", "180 days", "730 days"],
 "Publication 5 says originating functions may require more than 365 days to remain on the statute and may ask the taxpayer to extend it, or Appeals will not accept the case."),
("A formal protest signed by a representative who lacks personal knowledge of the facts must include:",
 "A perjury statement saying so", ["The client's notarized affidavit", "A copy of Form 8821", "The examiner's signature"],
 "Publication 5 gives a separate penalties of perjury declaration for a representative without personal knowledge, stating that he submitted the protest and has no personal knowledge of its contents."),
("Appeals cannot consider an argument based only on:",
 "Moral or political objections", ["Court decisions on the issue", "Hazards of litigating the case", "Facts the examiner overlooked"],
 "Publication 5 says Appeals can consider arguments based on tax law but not those based only on moral, religious, political, constitutional or similar objections to paying tax."),
("A client petitions the Tax Court without going to Appeals first. Her case will normally:",
 "Go to Appeals for settlement talks", ["Go straight to a trial date", "Be dismissed for skipping Appeals", "Return to the examiner first"],
 "Publication 556 says a case petitioned to the Tax Court will normally be considered for settlement by an Appeals officer before the court hears it."),
("Most cases not docketed in any court qualify for which Appeals service at the examination stage?",
 "Fast track mediation", ["Small tax case procedure", "Collection Appeals Program", "Audit reconsideration"],
 "Publication 556 says most non-docketed cases qualify for fast track mediation, in which a trained Appeals officer helps resolve disputes from exams, offers, TFRP cases and collection actions."),
("At an Appeals conference held without the client, the EA may receive confidential information only if she has:",
 "A POA or Form 8821 on file", ["The client's verbal consent", "Her enrollment card with her", "A copy of the 30-day letter"],
 "Publication 556 says a representative attending without the taxpayer can receive or inspect confidential information only if a power of attorney or tax information authorization is on file."),
("A client signs the agreement form after an audit but does not pay. To avoid more interest on a bill under $100,000, she pays within:",
 "21 calendar days of the bill", ["10 calendar days of the bill", "30 calendar days of the bill", "60 calendar days of the bill"],
 "Publication 556 says no more interest or penalties accrue if the bill is paid within 10 business days, extended to 21 calendar days when the amount due is less than $100,000."),
]
